import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a3 as o}from"./chunks/framework.D1GAkLkK.js";const l=JSON.parse('{"title":"How OpsTruss works","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/how-opstruss-works.md","filePath":"concepts/how-opstruss-works.md"}'),n={name:"concepts/how-opstruss-works.md"};function r(d,e,i,h,c,p){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="how-opstruss-works" tabindex="-1">How OpsTruss works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-opstruss-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How OpsTruss works&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="units-tasks-and-the-graph" tabindex="-1">Units, tasks, and the graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#units-tasks-and-the-graph" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Units, tasks, and the graph&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A project declares <strong>units</strong>, things that <code>up</code> brings up and <code>destroy</code> takes away, and <strong>tasks</strong>, actions that run when asked or when a trigger fires. A unit runs a program (<code>run</code>), keeps one running (<code>service</code>), or uses a plugin, a task, or a component (<code>uses</code>).</p><p>What a unit reads, such as <code>${{ units.db.outputs.host }}</code>, makes it come after that unit, as <code>needs:</code> does. OpsTruss builds a graph of the units from those references, runs the ones that do not depend on each other at once, and hands the typed outputs of one to the next: a number stays a number, a list stays a list.</p><h2 id="records-and-digests" tabindex="-1">Records and digests <a class="header-anchor" href="#records-and-digests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Records and digests&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each unit has a <strong>record</strong> in the state of its stack: the outputs of its last <code>up</code>, the program that destroys it, and a <strong>digest</strong> of its definition: its program, inputs, environment, and the content of its <code>sources</code>. <code>plan</code> compares the digest of the files with the record:</p><ul><li>a unit whose digest is the same is <code>unchanged</code>, unless a file it <code>generates</code> is missing;</li><li>a unit whose digest changed runs, and the plan says which part changed;</li><li>a plugin with a <code>plan</code> task decides for its unit, as <code>tofu plan</code> does.</li></ul><p>The record keeps the destroy program and its inputs, so <code>destroy</code>, and a unit that leaves the files, take away what was made, without the definition. A value that <code>pass:</code> let through is kept as a fingerprint: a destroy that would reach another target, such as another <code>AWS_PROFILE</code>, stops first.</p><p>Before a program of a unit runs, its record is marked <code>pending</code>. If OpsTruss is killed, the next run sees the mark, waits for the programs of the old run to end, and runs the unit again with <code>OPSTRUSS_REASON=interrupted</code>.</p><h2 id="plan-and-approval" tabindex="-1">Plan and approval <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-and-approval" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plan and approval&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>up</code> plans every unit first, shows the plan, and asks once at a terminal before it changes anything. In CI, <code>--yes</code> approves; without it, <code>up</code> stops at the plan and exits with 2. A plan that a plugin marks <strong>exact</strong> is the one that <code>up</code> applies: if the plan changed since the approval, the unit fails rather than apply something else.</p><p>Each address has a lock, so two runs never change one unit at once; a run waits for a lock that another holds, and says who holds it.</p><h2 id="stacks-and-state" tabindex="-1">Stacks and state <a class="header-anchor" href="#stacks-and-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stacks and state&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>stack</strong> is a copy of the units with its own inputs and its own state, such as <code>dev</code> and <code>prod</code>. This version keeps the state of each stack in <code>.opstruss/state/&lt;stack&gt;/</code> of the project, on the machine that runs it. Back it up: with the age key of the machine, it is the only copy.</p><h2 id="sensitive-values" tabindex="-1">Sensitive values <a class="header-anchor" href="#sensitive-values" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sensitive values&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A secret, and anything computed from it, is <strong>sensitive</strong>. OpsTruss hides it as <code>***</code> in every event, so the terminal, logs, records, and <code>--json</code> never show it, and encrypts it in the state with the data key of the stack. Each value reaches only the programs that name it in <code>env:</code>.</p><h2 id="the-daemon" tabindex="-1">The daemon <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-daemon" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The daemon&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Services and triggers need a process that stays: the <strong>daemon</strong> of the stack, which <code>up -d</code> starts. While it runs, the commands of that stack go to it, so a run from the command line and a run from a trigger share the same locks and the same view of the services.</p>',18)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{l as __pageData,g as default};
